                    <history>
                        <origin><origDate calendar="#Gregorian" notBefore="1100" notAfter="1499">s.
                                xii and s. xv ex</origDate></origin>
                        <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_86">Bury St
                                Edmunds, Suffolk, Benedictine abbey of St Edmund, King and
                                Martyr</orgName>
                            <p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval
                                Libraries of Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the
                                Supplement (1987). The corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great
                                Britain index card records only information about later ownership
                                (for which see below). The source of the attribution was likely Sir
                                George Warner, <title>Descriptive catalogue of illuminated
                                    manuscripts in the library of C. W. Dyson Perrins</title>
                                (Oxford, 1920), p. vii: <q>there is nothing to indicate the original
                                    provenance [...] though the later additions to it, which include
                                    an apparently unique Memoria and miniature of St. Robert the
                                    boy-martyr of Bury St. Edmunds, suggest that in the 15th century
                                    it was in that neighbourhood, if not at St. Edmund’s Abbey
                                    itself.</q></p></provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><q><persName>Susana Flint</persName>
                                and <persName>Joanes Pinchbeck</persName></q> (fol. 1, s.
                            xvi)</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><q><persName role="fmo">N.
                                    Roe</persName>, 1760</q> (fol. 1)</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Quaritch (sold to <persName role="fmo"
                                >Dyson Perrins</persName> 1916). Sold 1959, lot 55. This was the
                            status as reported in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great
                            Britain (1964). No update was provided in the Supplement
                            (1987).</provenance>
                        <provenance>Now </provenance>
                    </history>
